            <ArticleTitle>APOBEC Enzymes as Targets for Virus and Cancer Therapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2451-9456(17)30388-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.chembiol.2017.10.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Human DNA cytosine-to-uracil deaminases catalyze mutations in both pathogen and cellular genomes. APOBEC3D, APOBEC3F, APOBEC3G, and APOBEC3H restrict human immunodeficiency virus 1 (HIV-1) infection in cells deficient in the viral infectivity factor (Vif), and have the potential to catalyze sublethal levels of mutation in viral genomes in Vif-proficient cells. At least two APOBEC3 enzymes, and in particular APOBEC3B, are sources of somatic mutagenesis in cancer cells that drive tumor evolution and may manifest clinically as recurrence, metastasis, and/or therapy resistance. Consequently, APOBEC3 enzymes are tantalizing targets for developing chemical probes and therapeutic molecules to harness mutational processes in human disease. This review highlights recent efforts to chemically manipulate APOBEC3 activities.</AbstractText>
